	<string>https://learn.jamf.com/r/en-US/jamf-setup-reset-configuration-guide/Managed_App_Configuration_Reset</string>
	<key>pfm_domain</key>
	<string>com.jamf.reset</string>
	<key>pfm_format_version</key>
	<integer>1</integer>
	<key>pfm_interaction</key>
	<string>exclusive</string>
	<key>pfm_last_modified</key>
	<date>2026-09-08T00:00:00Z</date>
	<key>pfm_platforms</key>
	<array>
		<string>iOS</string>
		<string>iPadOS</string>
	</array>
	<key>pfm_subkeys</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>pfm_default</key>
			<string>Configures Jamf Reset</string>
			<key>pfm_description</key>
			<string>The human-readable description of this payload. This description appears on the Detail screen.</string>
			<key>pfm_name</key>
			<string>PayloadDescription</string>
			<key>pfm_title</key>
			<string>Payload Description</string>
			<key>pfm_type</key>
			<string>string</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>pfm_default</key>
			<string>Jamf Reset</string>
			<key>pfm_description</key>
			<string>The human-readable name for the profile payload. The name appears on the Detail screen and doesn’t need to be unique.</string>
			<key>pfm_name</key>
			<string>PayloadDisplayName</string>
			<key>pfm_require</key>
			<string>always</string>
			<key>pfm_title</key>
			<string>Payload Display Name</string>
			<key>pfm_type</key>
			<string>string</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>pfm_default</key>
			<string>com.jamf.reset</string>
			<key>pfm_description</key>
			<string>The reverse-DNS-style identifier for the payload. This identifier is usually the same as the TopLevel value, with an additional appended component. This string must be unique within the profile.
During a profile replacement, the system updates payloads with the same ‘PayloadIdentifier’ and ‘PayloadUUID’ in the old and new profiles.</string>
			<key>pfm_name</key>
			<string>PayloadIdentifier</string>
			<key>pfm_require</key>
			<string>always</string>
			<key>pfm_title</key>
			<string>Payload Identifier</string>
			<key>pfm_type</key>
			<string>string</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>pfm_default</key>
			<string>com.jamf.reset</string>
			<key>pfm_description</key>
			<string>The payload type, which each payload domain’s reference page specifies.</string>
			<key>pfm_name</key>
			<string>PayloadType</string>
			<key>pfm_require</key>
			<string>always</string>
			<key>pfm_title</key>
			<string>Payload Type</string>
			<key>pfm_type</key>
			<string>string</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>pfm_default</key>
			<string></string>
			<key>pfm_description</key>
			<string>The globally unique identifier for the payload. The actual content is unimportant, but must be globally unique. In macOS, use ‘uuidgen’ to generate UUIDs.
During a profile replacement, the system updates payloads with the same ‘PayloadIdentifier’ and ‘PayloadUUID’ in the old and new profiles.</string>
			<key>pfm_format</key>
			<string>^[0-9A-Za-z]{8}-[0-9A-Za-z]{4}-[0-9A-Za-z]{4}-[0-9A-Za-z]{4}-[0-9A-Za-z]{12}$</string>
			<key>pfm_name</key>
			<string>PayloadUUID</string>
			<key>pfm_require</key>
			<string>always</string>
			<key>pfm_title</key>
			<string>Payload UUID</string>
			<key>pfm_type</key>
			<string>string</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>pfm_default</key>
			<integer>1</integer>
			<key>pfm_description</key>
			<string>The version of this specific payload.</string>
			<key>pfm_name</key>
			<string>PayloadVersion</string>
			<key>pfm_range_list</key>
			<array>
				<integer>1</integer>
			</array>
			<key>pfm_require</key>
			<string>always</string>
			<key>pfm_title</key>
			<string>Payload Version</string>
			<key>pfm_type</key>
			<string>integer</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>pfm_description</key>
			<string>The human-readable string containing the name of the organization that provides the profile. This value doesn’t need to match the organization payload value in the enclosing dictionary.</string>
			<key>pfm_name</key>
			<string>PayloadOrganization</string>
			<key>pfm_title</key>
			<string>Payload Organization</string>
			<key>pfm_type</key>
			<string>string</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>pfm_description</key>
			<string>Base URL of the Jamf Pro server the device is enrolled in, including the scheme.

Enter the tenant root only — no trailing slash and no ‘/api’ path.

Example: https://your-tenant.jamfcloud.com</string>
			<key>pfm_format</key>
			<string>^https?://[^\s/]+(/[^\s]*)?$</string>
			<key>pfm_name</key>
			<string>com.jamf.config.jamfpro.url</string>
			<key>pfm_require</key>
			<string>always</string>
			<key>pfm_title</key>
			<string>Jamf Pro URL</string>
			<key>pfm_type</key>
			<string>string</string>
			<key>pfm_value_placeholder</key>
			<string>https://your-tenant.jamfcloud.com</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>pfm_default</key>
			<string>$JSSID</string>
			<key>pfm_description</key>
			<string>The device’s Jamf Pro record ID, which Jamf Setup uses to write the selection back to inventory.

Leave this as the Jamf Pro variable $JSSID so the ID is substituted at install time.</string>
			<key>pfm_name</key>
			<string>com.jamf.config.jamfpro.device-id</string>
			<key>pfm_note</key>
			<string>If devices show a literal $JSSID, the value is not being delivered as App Configuration — Jamf Pro substitutes variables only in the app’s configuration, not in a static payload pasted elsewhere.</string>
			<key>pfm_range_list</key>
			<array>
				<string>$JSSID</string>
			</array>
			<key>pfm_range_list_allow_custom_value</key>
			<true/>
			<key>pfm_range_list_titles</key>
			<array>
				<string>$JSSID (substituted by Jamf Pro)</string>
			</array>
			<key>pfm_require</key>
			<string>always</string>
			<key>pfm_title</key>
			<string>Jamf Pro Device ID</string>
			<key>pfm_type</key>
			<string>string</string>
			<key>pfm_value_placeholder</key>
			<string>$JSSID</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>pfm_default</key>
			<string>$OAUTH_AUTH_CODE</string>
			<key>pfm_description</key>
			<string>Authorisation code that enables OAuth between Jamf Setup and Jamf Pro.

Leave this as the Jamf Pro variable $OAUTH_AUTH_CODE; Jamf Pro substitutes a per-device code at install time.</string>
			<key>pfm_name</key>
			<string>com.jamf.config.oauth.authorization-code</string>
			<key>pfm_note</key>
			<string>Jamf Setup 3.0.0 and later no longer support basic authentication. Set this key — not Jamf Pro Username and Password — on any current deployment.</string>
			<key>pfm_range_list</key>
			<array>
				<string>$OAUTH_AUTH_CODE</string>
			</array>
			<key>pfm_range_list_titles</key>
			<array>
				<string>$OAUTH_AUTH_CODE (substituted by Jamf Pro)</string>
			</array>
			<key>pfm_require</key>
			<string>always</string>
			<key>pfm_title</key>
			<string>OAuth Authorization Code</string>
			<key>pfm_type</key>
			<string>string</string>
			<key>pfm_value_placeholder</key>
			<string>$OAUTH_AUTH_CODE</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>pfm_default</key>
			<string>$APP_CONFIG_REINSTALL_CODE</string>
			<key>pfm_description</key>
			<string>Authorisation code that lets Jamf Pro reinstall the app configuration on a device that already has one.

Leave this as the Jamf Pro variable $APP_CONFIG_REINSTALL_CODE.</string>
			<key>pfm_name</key>
			<string>com.jamf.config.reinstall-code</string>
			<key>pfm_range_list</key>
			<array>
				<string>$APP_CONFIG_REINSTALL_CODE</string>
			</array>
			<key>pfm_range_list_titles</key>
			<array>
				<string>$APP_CONFIG_REINSTALL_CODE (substituted by Jamf Pro)</string>
			</array>
			<key>pfm_title</key>
			<string>App Config Reinstall Code</string>
			<key>pfm_type</key>
			<string>string</string>
			<key>pfm_value_placeholder</key>
			<string>$APP_CONFIG_REINSTALL_CODE</string>
